{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>O Adonai, Elohim, You have begun to show Your servant Your greatness, etc. Let me cross over, I pray, and see.</b> One should wonder about this prayer and supplication of the master of the prophets — how it coheres and what the connection of one part to the other is: for he begins his account with the mention of the wonders and mighty acts of the Holy One, blessed be He, and seals his request and supplication with 'Let me cross over, I pray, and see.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): 'You have begun' — he explains the matter of the giving of the Torah, which is the ultimate purpose sought from man. Therefore he said 'Your servant,' for he is the faithful servant who merited the receiving of the Torah that was given through him. 'Your greatness' — after what is written concerning the giving of the Torah (Deuteronomy 5:21): את כבודו ואת גדלו. 'And Your strong hand' — after what is written (ibid.): ואת קולו שמענו מתוך האש. 'For who is El in heaven and earth' — after what is written (Deuteronomy 4:36): מן השמים השמיעך את קולו ליסרך ועל הארץ הראך את אשו הגדולה. 'Who does like Your deeds in heaven' — 'and like Your might in earth,' and this is why he said at the beginning of his speech two holy Names, after what is written (Exodus 20:2): אנכי ה׳ אלהיך. And the verses are connected thus: 'You have begun to give Torah through me, and the essence of the Torah is only in the Land,' and one who begins a commandment is told, 'Complete it.' If so — 'Let me cross over, I pray, and see.' Thus explained my master, the great rabbi R. Sh. N.R.V." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain the coherence of the words of this prayer and supplication thus: Moses includes in the totality of his words the totality of all the points, which are the principles of all existence, above and below — for he had already attained the middle line above; therefore he asked to see with the physical eye the middle line below, to attain that which corresponds to it. Therefore he says: 'You have begun' — and given me power and apprehension to grasp the middle point from which all the branches emanate and are woven; and it is like a point within a circle, which is the beginning of the circle. 'For who is El in heaven and earth' — which is the point within the heavens, namely the spheres that surround it. Therefore he said 'who does like Your deeds and like Your might,' for the might of the Holy One, blessed be He, is proclaimed when the world is sustained through the movement of the spheres; and thus the language of Scripture (Psalms 19:2): ומעשה ידיו מגיד הרקיע. Therefore he says 'Let me cross over, I pray, and see' — since You have granted me knowledge to apprehend the middle point above, let Your kindness be great with me that I may see the good Land, which is the middle point below. 'The good mountain' is Jerusalem, which is the middle line of the good Land, and 'the Lebanon' is the Temple, which is the middle line of the good mountain. He means to say: since the One above has shown and enlightened him with the eye of the intellect, it is fitting that He not conceal from him the lower realm, but rather grant him to come there to see it, in order that he may add merit upon his merit through the fulfillment of the commandments that depend there; and through the lower his soul will be elevated to the higher, to shine in the light of life in that degree prepared for one who has fulfilled all the commandments in their entirety, unto perfection." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'You have begun' — would You decree upon me that I not enter the Land except on account of (Numbers 20:10) שמעו נא המורים? 'You have begun' — You who said (ibid. 17): למשמרת לאות לבני מרי." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> Adonai YHWH, You have begun to show in the bush, Your servant Your greatness and Your strong hand — the גדולה and the גבורה. And so was the beginning of my prophecy, and You made known to the creatures through this that there is no Elohim in heaven and earth who does like Your deeds and like Your might; and this is what David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 86): אין כמוך באלהים אדני ואין כמעשיך. Therefore 'let Your kindness be great with me' and 'Let me cross over, I pray, and see,' etc. And since He is 'the master of those who turn the rock into pools of water,' and Moses sinned against Him in the matter of the Rock, and it was decreed upon him on that account that he not enter the Land — therefore Moses now asks before Him that He forgive him and let him cross over and see. For this reason this Name is written אל״ף דל״ת, and after it יו״ד ה״א וא״ו ה״א, for He is Adonai, in whom is mercy — for ביה ה׳. Therefore he used the expression ואתחנן, which is an expression used of the attribute of judgment, as in (Psalms 30): אליך ה׳ אקרא ואל אדני אתחנן — he attached the supplication to אל״ף דל״ת. And likewise it is written (Job 8:5): אם אתה תשחר אל אל ואל שדי תתחנן."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:19.664654793Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}